course: dictionary
lesson: 6997
title: Paradox: two things that seem impossible
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A paradox is a statement, idea or situation that seems to contradict itself or seems impossible, but may still be true. It is a noun, so you can have one paradox or several paradoxes.
explain: Look for the clue that something does not fit. «Travel into the past is not known to be possible, and it raises puzzles called paradoxes, like changing something that already happened.» The puzzle is that if you changed the past, the event that made your trip possible might never have happened.
explain: Paradoxes can also appear in everyday life. "The more you rush, the slower you go" sounds wrong at first, but it points out something strange and worth thinking about. Ask yourself: why do both parts seem true at the same time?
fact: A paradox seems impossible or contradictory, but it can still be true.
fact: «Paradox» is a noun: a paradox, paradoxes.
fact: "This sentence is false" is a famous paradox from logic: if it is true, then it must be false, and if it is false, then it must be true.
fact: The grandfather paradox asks what would happen if you travelled back in time and stopped your own grandfather from meeting your grandmother.
fact: Paradoxes are not a sign that you are wrong to think hard; they test careful thinking, and olympiad puzzles often use them.
exercise: «Travel into the past is not known to be possible, and it raises puzzles called paradoxes.» What is a paradox? | *An idea that seems to contradict itself but may still be true | A sum with only one possible answer | A very old castle | A fast way to travel
exercise: "This sentence is false" is a famous puzzle. Why is it a paradox? | It is written in capital letters | It is a sentence with only three words | It has no verb | *If it is true, it must be false, and if it is false, it must be true
exercise: "The more you rush, the slower you go." Why might this be called a paradox? | *It sounds impossible, yet it points out something strange that may be true | It is a question about the weather | It is a sum with an answer of zero | It is a clear rule about running fast
test: Which of these is a paradox? | A list of animals | A map of the school | A plain sum like 2 + 2 | *A puzzle where reasoning seems to lead to an impossible answer
test: What is the plural of paradox? | paradix | *paradoxes | paradoxies | paradoxs
exam: Choose the best description of a paradox: | A puzzle that is never true and has no meaning | A word for something that is always easy to understand | *A statement or situation that seems impossible or contradictory, but may still be true | A word meaning very old
